Author:     Pingzii <109567708+Pingzii@users.noreply.github.com>
AuthorDate: Mon Oct 26 22:01:04 2026 +0800
Commit:     GitHub <noreply@github.com>
CommitDate: Mon Oct 26 22:01:04 2026 +0800

    [BugFix][Platform] Declare the KV cache dtypes DeepSeek V4 can store (#18891)
    
    ### What this PR does / why we need it?
    
    A DeepSeek V4 launch on A5 with no `--kv-cache-dtype` used to die inside
    the
    operator:
    
    aclnnMixedQuantSparseFlashMla: Parameter ori_kv head dimension ... value
    512.
        It should be 608.
    
    A5 picks the KV layout from the literal `cache_config.cache_dtype`:
    `bfloat16`
    takes the SparseFlashMla plan and skips the fp8 compress epilog,
    everything else
    takes the epilog
    (`vllm_ascend/attention/dsa_attn_kv_plan.py:is_a5_bf16_kv_enabled`).
    `auto` means the model dtype, bfloat16, but the literal matches neither
    branch, so
    the plan asked for a fp8 page (`head_size + 96`, i.e. 608) while vLLM
    built a
    bfloat16 one. The closed #18494 recorded the same misconfiguration from
    another
    angle:
    
    RuntimeError: view size is not compatible with input tensor's size and
    stride ...
    
    #### The resolution
    
    `is_a5_bf16_kv_enabled` is where the layout is decided, and it read the
    literal
    `cache_dtype` only. An unset request means the model dtype, bfloat16, so
    it now selects
    the same layout an explicit bfloat16 request does. Nothing upstream of
    it changes a
    value -- the page is bfloat16 either way, and the plan that met it was
    the wrong one.
    
    #### The declaration
    
    With that, the dtypes DeepSeek V4 can store are declarable.
    `AscendDSABackend`
    states them -- the way
    `vllm/models/deepseek_v4/nvidia/flashinfer_sparse.py` splits SM10x from
    SM12x -- and
    answers upstream's two questions per device:
    
    A5 auto, bfloat16, fp8 compressed cache is fp8, or bfloat16 through the
                                     SparseFlashMla plan
    A3 auto, bfloat16, int8 no compressed cache; the MLA cache is stored as
    int8
    
    `fp8_e4m3`, `fp8_e5m2`, `mxfp8` and `nvfp4` have no Ascend build on
    either
    part, so a request for one is refused where it is set instead of failing
    inside the
    operator:
    
    ValueError: KV cache dtype 'fp8_e4m3' is not supported by the attention
    backend
        AscendDSASWABackend. Reasons: kv_cache_dtype not supported.
        Supported kv_cache_dtype values: auto, bfloat16, fp8.
    
    The check runs from two places, because Ascend selects backends from
    two. The
    standard layers go through `NPUPlatform.get_attn_backend_cls`, which
    asks those two
    questions against the backend the lookup table returned.
    `validate_configuration`
    is not used there: it needs the whole selector config through
    `_asdict()`, and
    `use_compress` is not a field of `AttentionSelectorConfig`, so callers
    that set it
    build a `SimpleNamespace` (`tests/ut/attention/test_flash_attn.py`). The
    DeepSeek
    sparse layers assign `AscendDSAC4Backend` / `AscendDSAC128Backend` /
    `AscendDSASWABackend` directly and never reach the platform selector, so
    they ask
    the same two questions where they pick their backend.
    
    Only backends that declare the set are checked -- the inherited upstream
    default is a
    CUDA-oriented set that would reject configurations working today -- and
    a backend
    whose module cannot be imported is skipped: the FA3 backend needs
    `flash_attn_npu_v3`, absent on CPU runners.
    
    #### The indexer KV dtype
    
    The KV cache dtype is not the only dtype crossing into the compressed
    cache. The
    DeepSeek V4 indexer K cache has no bf16 write path either, and
    `indexer_kv_dtype="auto"` had no platform default, so it resolved to
    bf16 and
    disagreed with the cache for the same reason. Resolve it to the dtype
    each part
    implements and reject the rest up front, the way upstream's sparse
    indexer does with
    `DSA_INDEXER_KV_DTYPES`:
    
    ValueError: indexer_kv_dtype='int8' is not supported by the DeepSeek V4
        indexer on this platform (expected one of ('fp8',)).
    
    ### Does this PR introduce _any_ user-facing change?
    
    Yes, on A5 and A3. A DeepSeek V4 launch without `--kv-cache-dtype` now
    starts
    instead of failing inside the operator during graph capture, and a dtype
    neither
    Ascend part can build is refused while the layers are being built, with
    the values
    that work.
    
    ### How was this patch tested?
    
    `tests/ut/test_kv_cache_dtype_validation.py` covers the declaration, the
    per-device
    narrowing on A5 and A3, the resolution, the entry point (including a
    selector config
    that is not a `NamedTuple`), the sparse layer's own check, the opt-in
    inheritance
    through subclasses, the skip for undeclared and unimportable backends,
    and the
    message. `tests/ut/attention/test_dsa_attn_kv_plan.py` covers the
    resolution itself,
    and the indexer tests sit next to the rest of the DeepSeek V4 indexer
    tests.
    
    A5 real machine, DeepSeek-V4-Flash, DP4 + EP, block size 32:
    
    - no `--kv-cache-dtype` -> **starts and serves** (this is the fix);
    - `--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16` -> starts and serves;
    - `--kv-cache-dtype fp8` -> starts and serves;
    - `--kv-cache-dtype fp8_e4m3` -> refused with the message above;
    - `--kv-cache-dtype int8` -> refused on A5 (A3's dtype);
    - `--attention-config '{"indexer_kv_dtype": "int8"}'` -> the indexer
    `ValueError`
      above;
    - before this patch the same default launch reached ACLGraph capture and
    failed with
      the operator error quoted at the top.
    
    (Those runs also need `--block-size 32`; without it, block_size is
    resolved from
    the backend list up to 4096 and the DeepSeek block-size table raises
    `KeyError: 4096`. That is a separate issue, tracked separately.)
    
    - vLLM main:
    https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/commit/c0b6f74bee518aeaf5d72b200a0216e72fbb5e7c
    
    Signed-off-by: 宋元盟 <songyuanmeng1@huawei.com>
    Co-authored-by: 宋元盟 <songyuanmeng1@huawei.com>

